===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{term|actus reus||guilty act|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|āctus||act}} + {{term|reus||guilty|lang=la}}, after {{term|mens rea|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
*{{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌaktəsˈɹeɪəs/|/ˌaktəsˈɹiːəs/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|actus rei}}

# {{context|legal|lang=en}} A [[criminal]] act, considered as separate from the intentions or state of mind of the [[perpetrator]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2011|author={{w|Steven Pinker}}|title=The Better Angels of Our Nature|publisher=Penguin 2012|page=610|passage=A brute emotional reflex to the '''''actus reus''''', the bad act (‘She killed her husband! Shame!’), could trigger an urge for retribution regardless of her intention.}}

